 ## Overview
-Hermite Data Computation via Bisection is a method for precisely locating isosurface intersections on the edges of an adaptive octree's leaf cells and computing associated surface normals [S1, unofficial]. It solves the problem of geometric inaccuracy in dual contouring, where linear interpolation between corner values introduces visible jaggedness on curved or sharp features [S1, unofficial]. Unlike [Octree Path Computation](897-octree-path-computation.md) which focuses on traversal efficiency, or [Sparse Voxel Octree Ray Traversal](2451-sparse-voxel-octree-ray-traversal.md) which samples volume density, this method refines intersection positions to sub-cell accuracy to enable accurate Quadratic Error Function (QEF) vertex placement [S1, unofficial].
+Hermite Data Computation via Bisection is a method for finding all edge crossings in an adaptive octree for an implicit function and computing Hermite data (intersection points and normals) by refining linear interpolation guesses with bisection [S1, unofficial]. It differs from its sibling [Tree Marcher](7534-tree-marcher.md) by providing high-precision intersection locations necessary for dual contouring, whereas Tree Marcher typically focuses on marching through the grid to generate triangles directly. It differs from [Tetrahedralization of Octree Cells](9049-tetrahedralization-of-octree-cells.md) by operating on the octree's edge structure rather than subdividing cells into tetrahedra, allowing it to handle adaptive depth boundaries without geometric artifacts [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates on the leaf cells of an adaptive octree [S1, unofficial]. It identifies edges where the implicit function changes sign relative to an iso-value, refines the intersection point along these edges using bisection, and computes normals [S1, unofficial].
-
